{"subjectId":"c","id":"loops","title":"Loops: for, while, do-while","group":"Control Flow & Functions","order":14,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Three loop forms, plus <code>break</code>, <code>continue</code> and (rarely) <code>goto</code>. Pick <code>for</code> when you count, <code>while</code> when you wait for a condition, <code>do-while</code> when the body must run at least once.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-refresh\"></i>The three loops</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++)          /* init; condition; step */\n        printf(\"%d \", i);\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n\n    int n = 1234, digits = 0;\n    while (n != 0) {                     /* test before each iteration */\n        n /= 10;\n        digits++;\n    }\n    printf(\"digits = %d\\n\", digits);\n\n    int k = 100;\n    do {                                 /* body runs at least once */\n        printf(\"k = %d\\n\", k);\n    } while (k &lt; 10);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>0 1 2 3 4\ndigits = 4\nk = 100</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-player-skip-forward\"></i>break and continue</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 10; i++) {\n        if (i % 2 == 0) continue;   /* skip even numbers */\n        if (i &gt; 7) break;           /* leave the loop entirely */\n        printf(\"%d \", i);\n    }\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1 3 5 7</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-grid-dots\"></i>Nested loops and patterns</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n = 4;\n    for (int row = 1; row &lt;= n; row++) {\n        for (int s = 0; s &lt; n - row; s++) putchar('.');\n        for (int star = 0; star &lt; 2 * row - 1; star++) putchar('*');\n        putchar('\\n');\n    }\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>...*\n..***\n.*****\n*******</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-back-up\"></i>Breaking out of nested loops: goto</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int grid[3][3] = {{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}, {7, 8, 9}};\n    int target = 5;\n    for (int r = 0; r &lt; 3; r++)\n        for (int c = 0; c &lt; 3; c++)\n            if (grid[r][c] == target) {\n                printf(\"found at (%d,%d)\\n\", r, c);\n                goto done;              /* one of the few acceptable uses */\n            }\n    printf(\"not found\\n\");\ndone:\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>found at (1,1)</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">goto</div><p><code>goto</code> is legitimate in C for two idioms: leaving deeply nested loops and the <em>single cleanup exit</em> pattern (<code>goto cleanup;</code> to free resources in reverse order — used throughout the Linux kernel). Never jump backwards to build loops.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list\"></i>Loop idioms</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Idiom</th><th>Code</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Infinite loop</td><td><code>for (;;) { … }</code> or <code>while (1) { … }</code></td></tr><tr><td>Iterate an array</td><td><code>for (size_t i = 0; i &lt; n; i++)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Count down with <code>size_t</code></td><td><code>for (size_t i = n; i-- &gt; 0; )</code> (avoids the unsigned wrap trap)</td></tr><tr><td>Read until EOF</td><td><code>while ((c = getchar()) != EOF)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Two indexes</td><td><code>for (int i = 0, j = n - 1; i &lt; j; i++, j--)</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a[] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};\n    size_t n = sizeof a / sizeof a[0];\n    for (size_t i = n; i-- &gt; 0; )           /* safe reverse loop with unsigned index */\n        printf(\"%d \", a[i]);\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    for (int i = 0, j = (int)n - 1; i &lt; j; i++, j--) {   /* reverse in place */\n        int t = a[i]; a[i] = a[j]; a[j] = t;\n    }\n    for (size_t i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) printf(\"%d \", a[i]);\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>5 4 3 2 1\n5 4 3 2 1</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Off-by-one: <code>for (i = 0; i &lt;= n; i++)</code> over an array of <code>n</code> elements touches <code>a[n]</code> — one past the end. That is a buffer overflow (see Memory Bugs).</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between while and do-while?</summary><div><p><code>while</code> checks the condition <em>before</em> each iteration, so the body may run zero times. <code>do-while</code> checks <em>after</em>, so the body always runs at least once — useful for menus and input validation loops.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why is for (unsigned i = n - 1; i &gt;= 0; i--) an infinite loop?</summary><div><p>An unsigned value is always <code>&gt;= 0</code>; when <code>i</code> is 0, <code>i--</code> wraps to <code>UINT_MAX</code>. Use a signed index or the <code>for (size_t i = n; i-- &gt; 0; )</code> idiom.</p></div></details></section>"}